Excellent choice. Group 1 is the most critical foundation to fix first, because if Hibernate runs with the current configuration, it could corrupt your database schema.
Here are the exact code replacements you need to make for the **Entities & Configs (Group 1)**.
### 1. Fix Missing Entity Fields (Critical to prevent schema deletion)
If Hibernate updates your database right now, it will delete the INR_BALANCE and CURRENCY_RATE columns. We need to add them to your Java models.
**File:** GlBalance.java
Find the balance field and add the inrBalance field right after it:
```java
    @Column(name = "BALANCE", precision = 25, scale = 4)
    private BigDecimal balance;

    // ✅ ADD THIS:
    @Column(name = "INR_BALANCE", precision = 25, scale = 4)
    private BigDecimal inrBalance;

```
**File:** CurrencyMaster.java
Find the flag field and add the currencyRate field right after it:
```java
    @Column(name = "FLAG")
    private Integer flag;

    // ✅ ADD THIS:
    @Column(name = "CURRENCY_RATE", precision = 18, scale = 6)
    private BigDecimal currencyRate;

```
### 2. Lock Down Hibernate Schema Generation
Never let Hibernate alter your production tables automatically. We must change it to only "validate" what is there.
**File:** OracleDbConfig.java
Locate your oracleEntityManagerFactory() method and change the hbm2ddl.auto property.
**Change this:**
```java
properties.put("hibernate.hbm2ddl.auto", "update");

```
**To this:**
```java
properties.put("hibernate.hbm2ddl.auto", "validate"); // ✅ FIX: Validates schema instead of altering it

```
**
### 3. Remove Illegal @Temporal Annotations
@Temporal is an outdated annotation for old Java Date objects and will cause Hibernate errors when used with modern java.time classes.
**File:** GlTransaction.java (and check JournalRequest.java if it has the same)
**Change this:**
```java
    @Column(name = "TRANSACTION_DATE")
    @Temporal(TemporalType.DATE)
    private LocalDate transactionDate;

    @Column(name = "POST_DATE")
    @Temporal(TemporalType.TIMESTAMP)
    private LocalDateTime postDate;

```
**To this:**
```java
    // ✅ FIX: Removed @Temporal annotations
    @Column(name = "TRANSACTION_DATE")
    private LocalDate transactionDate;

    @Column(name = "POST_DATE")
    private LocalDateTime postDate;

```
**
### 4. Fix Validation in DTOs
Using @Column in a DTO class does absolutely nothing. We need to use jakarta.validation.constraints to actually enforce the rules.
**File:** ProcessJournalRequestDto.java
**Change this:**
```java
@Data
@Getter
@Setter
public class ProcessJournalRequestDto {
    @Column(nullable = false)
    private Long requestId;
    @Column(nullable = false)
    private RequestStatus status;
    @Size(max = 50)
    private String remarks;
}

```
**To this:**
```java
@Data
public class ProcessJournalRequestDto {
    // ✅ FIX: Replaced @Column with @NotNull for proper validation, and removed redundant @Getter/@Setter
    @NotNull(message = "Request ID is required")
    private Long requestId;
    
    @NotNull(message = "Status is required")
    private RequestStatus status;
    
    @Size(max = 50)
    private String remarks;
}

```
